#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

struct Fill {
    double qty;
    explicit Fill(double q) : qty(q) { std::cout << "Fill " << q << " created\n"; }
    ~Fill() { std::cout << "Fill " << qty << " destroyed\n"; }
};

std::unique_ptr<Fill> book_fill(double qty) {
    return std::make_unique<Fill>(qty);   // move/RVO: no copy occurs
}

int main() {
    auto a = std::make_unique<Fill>(100);
    auto b = std::move(a);                 // ownership: a -> b
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << "a null? " << (a == nullptr) << '\n';
    std::cout << "b holds " << b->qty << '\n';
    auto c = book_fill(250);
    c.reset();                             // destroy now
    std::cout << "end of main\n";
}                                          // b's Fill destroyed here
// Fill 100 created
// a null? true
// b holds 100
// Fill 250 created
// Fill 250 destroyed
// end of main
// Fill 100 destroyed

#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

struct Session { int id; };

int main() {
    auto closer = [](Session* s) {
        std::cout << "closing session " << s->id << '\n';
        delete s;
    };
    std::unique_ptr<Session, decltype(closer)> s(new Session{7}, closer);
    std::cout << "session live\n";
}   // custom deleter runs here
// session live
// closing session 7

#include <memory>
#include <cstdio>

struct Book { double px; explicit Book(double p) : px(p) { puts("book built"); }
               ~Book() { puts("book gone"); } };

std::unique_ptr<Book> quote_factory(double px) {
    auto b = std::make_unique<Book>(px);   // single allocation, exception-safe
    return b;                              // NRVO / move: ownership leaves the function
}

int main() {
    auto h = quote_factory(101.25);        // h now owns the Book
    // auto h2 = h;                       // COMPILE ERROR: copy is deleted
    auto h2 = std::move(h);               // ownership transferred; h is now null
    if (!h) puts("h is empty after move");
    printf("px = %.2f\n", h2->px);
    return 0;                             // h2 goes out of scope: destructor runs here
}

guidedExercise.solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

struct Order { int id; };

std::unique_ptr<Order> load_order() {
    return std::make_unique<Order>();
}

void risk_stage(std::unique_ptr<Order> o) {   // sink: takes ownership
    std::cout << "risk owns order " << o->id << '\n';
}                                             // o destroyed here

int main() {
    auto o = load_order();
    std::cout << "main owns order\n";
    risk_stage(std::move(o));                 // hand-off
    std::cout << "main still owns it? " << std::boolalpha << (o != nullptr) << '\n';
}
```

Output:

```
main owns order
risk owns order
main still owns it? false
```

load_order's return value is moved into main's o (elided). The std::move hands ownership into the sink parameter, which is destroyed at the end of risk_stage - destroying the Order. Back in main, o is in the moved-from state: null, safe to assign or reset, never to dereference.

independentExercise.prompt:
This function leaks on its error path:

```cpp
void stage(int id) {
    int* ctx = new int(id);
    if (id % 2 == 0) throw std::runtime_error("even id");
    delete ctx;
}
```

(a) Rewrite it with std::unique_ptr so no path leaks. (b) State the exact moved-from rules for unique_ptr: which operations are safe and which are not. (c) The team wants to keep the raw pointer around for logging after the unique_ptr goes out of scope - explain why `release()` is the wrong tool and what would happen.

independentExercise.solution:
(a) ```cpp
#include <memory>
void stage(int id) {
    auto ctx = std::make_unique<int>(id);   // RAII: freed on every path
    if (id % 2 == 0) throw std::runtime_error("even id");
}
``` The throw triggers unwinding; ctx's destructor deletes the int. No leak is possible. (b) A moved-from (or released-from) unique_ptr is **valid but null**: safe to assign (`p = std::make_unique<...>()`), safe to reset, safe to destroy, safe to compare against nullptr; unsafe to dereference (operator* / ->) and unsafe to call get() and use the result as an owner. (c) `release()` surrenders ownership to you: the object is *not* destroyed, and the returned raw pointer now carries the delete obligation. If the code just logs it and leaves scope, that is a leak - exactly the bug unique_ptr was introduced to prevent. For observation, use `.get()` (no ownership change) while the unique_ptr is alive.

